        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Mimic simple actions shown in picture books.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can mimic body language to convey polite expressions from picture books (e.g., nodding and smiling to say "thank you").
Can act out simple greetings shown in picture books (e.g., waving or saying "hello" to show meeting a new friend).
Can role-play basic greeting exchanges from picture books (e.g., greeting classmates with "hello").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage &amp; Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities &amp; Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Hello, Friends!" song with actions. Students wave their hands and say "Hello, friend!" and then say their own name: "I am [name]." The teacher models waving and smiling. This activates prior knowledge of greetings and names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards depicting polite actions and expressions (e.g., a child waving and saying "hello," a child smiling and nodding to say "thank you"). The teacher explicitly models the actions and says the words clearly: "This is 'hello'," (waves), "This is 'thank you'," (nods and smiles). The teacher emphasizes syllable stress in "friends" and "goodbye" and the initial /f/ in "friend" and /g/ in "goodbye" through repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher waves) "Is this 'hello'?" (Expected response: Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher pretends to receive a toy and smiles) "Do we say 'thank you' when we get a nice toy?" (Expected response: Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple picture book titled "Friends Say Hello and Thank You" featuring clear illustrations of children interacting, waving, saying "hello," and showing gratitude. (Genre: Narrative excerpt, Register: Informal, Format: Picture book).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and interpret simple actions and polite expressions presented visually in a picture book, connecting them to physical miming and basic verbal responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the picture book, pointing to the pictures. For each page showing a greeting or polite expression, the teacher asks, "What do the friends say?" (e.g., "Hello!"). Students listen and point to the word "hello" or "thank you" in the book if visible, or to the action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture (e.g., two friends waving). Students mimic the action (wave their hands) and say "hello." The teacher then shows a picture of a friend giving another a toy, and the receiver smiles. Students mimic smiling and say "thank you." This directly practices miming actions from the picture book.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill &amp; Content:</b> Students mediate the meaning of social cues by acting out polite expressions ("hello," "goodbye," "thank you," "please") and answering simple WH-questions ("What is your name?"). Emphasis on grammar: Subject + Verb ("I am [name].") and vocabulary: nouns (friend, name), verbs (say, wave, meet), adjectives (nice, happy), adverb (please), interjections (hello, goodbye, thank you). Pronunciation focus on initial /f/ and /g/ and syllable stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Action &amp; Word Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays a set of large flashcards, each showing an action (waving, nodding, a happy face, a hand offering something). The teacher shows a card and asks, "What do we say?" or "What do we do?" Students then mimic the action and say the corresponding word (e.g., "hello" for waving, "thank you" for nodding, "please" for offering). The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of "friend," "hello," "goodbye," and "thank you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Scenario Mime (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher gives simple verbal scenarios (e.g., "You meet a new friend!"). Students respond by miming the appropriate action (wave) and saying the greeting ("Hello!"). Other scenarios: "You are leaving class" (mime waving "goodbye"), "Someone gives you a cookie" (mime smiling and say "Thank you!"). The teacher prompts with "What do you say?" or "What do you do?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Greeting Train (Pair Interaction):</b> Students stand in a line. The first student turns to the next, waves, and says "Hello, [friend's name]!" (practicing "I am [name]," "What is your name?" from previous lessons). The second student responds and then turns to the next. The teacher facilitates, correcting pronunciation of "friends" and "goodbye" where needed and reinforcing "please" when asking for a turn.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Friendship Storybook Show and Tell" with actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students will present one or two pages from their simple "Friendship Storybook" (which they created or started in previous lessons). For each page, they will point to a picture of friends interacting and *mediate* its meaning by mimicking the action shown and saying the appropriate polite expression or greeting. For example, if a page shows two friends waving, the student will wave and say "Hello, friend!" If a page shows a child receiving something and smiling, the student will smile and say "Thank you!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle with their "Friendship Storybooks."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models first: "This is my book! See? Friends say 'hello'!" (waves). "And friends say 'thank you'!" (smiles and nods).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes a turn to show a chosen page from their storybook. They point to the picture and then perform the action depicted while saying the corresponding word or phrase (e.g., "hello," "goodbye," "thank you," "nice friend").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages classmates to echo the actions or words when appropriate, fostering collaborative interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually present one page from their "Friendship Storybook," pointing to a picture and mediating its meaning by performing the action and saying the related polite expression (e.g., waving for "hello," nodding for "thank you").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist - Yes/No):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mimics body language to convey "hello" (e.g., waving).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mimics body language to convey "thank you" (e.g., nodding/smiling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary "hello" intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary "thank you" intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Points to the relevant picture in their storybook.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a "hello" flashcard) "Can you show me 'hello' in your book? And say 'hello'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher models smiling and nodding) "Can you show me how to say 'thank you'?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like showing your 'Friendship Storybook'?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you wave and say 'hello'?" (Students wave).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this mediation lesson for Pre-K students? The visual input from the picture book and the "Friendship Storybook" project effectively engaged students and provided a concrete context for miming and simple verbalization. The consistent use of TPR (Total Physical Response) helped bridge the gap between visual information and physical/verbal output. Students responded well to the "Greeting Train" and the show-and-tell format, allowing them to practice "hello" and "thank you" in a meaningful way.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students needed more individual prompting to connect the picture to the action and the word. Breaking down the presentation into showing *one* action/word per turn might help. For future mediation tasks at this level, I will ensure even more explicit modeling of the transition from seeing an image to performing an action and saying the word. Incorporating more songs or chants that involve direct action-word pairing could also reinforce learning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson in the "Meeting My Friends" theme. The next lesson will begin a new theme, building on the foundational communication skills (greetings, politeness) established in this unit, potentially introducing new vocabulary and scenarios.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr></tbody></table>